<TopicPage slug="subjects/art-history/advanced/modern-and-contemporary-art">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Modern and contemporary art is the <strong>relentless interrogation of its own
    premises</strong> — from Cubism\'s shattered space to conceptual art\'s dematerialised
    object. It is the hardest period to love casually and the most rewarding to read: every move
    is an argument with every previous move.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the cascade, New York, dematerialisation, the expanded field" widget="equationStep" config={modernCfg} />

  <h2>Reading strategies that work</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Ask what question the work answers:</strong> the theory lesson\'s frames made period-specific — a Pollock answers "what is a painting?" (an event), a Warhol answers "what is celebrity?" (a surface), a Kusama answers "what is perception?" (an infinity). The question, not the object, is the entry point.</li>
    <li><strong>The readymade changed everything:</strong> Duchamp\'s urinal (1917) relocated art from making to choosing — the curator, the context and the concept became the materials; every conceptual work since is a footnote with better lighting.</li>
    <li><strong>Don\'t skip the wall text, then argue with it:</strong> contemporary art often requires the label (the context is part of the work) — but the critique lesson\'s discipline applies: does the piece deliver what the text promises? The gap is frequently the honest review.</li>
    <li><strong>Biennales and the global turn:</strong> contemporary art\'s map is no longer New York–Europe: Lagos, Seoul, São Paulo, Sharjah — the globalisation lesson\'s flows, in the art world\'s own economy (which the next lesson prices).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> the art-history walk-through lesson; the theory lesson\'s frames; O\'Doherty, Inside the White Cube (the gallery as medium).</p>
</TopicPage>
